  "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $60%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $40%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.60,\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=0.40.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.10.\n$$\n\n- Since $12%$ of the components produced are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|{A}^{\u2032}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 0.12 & =(0.10)(0.60)+P(D|{A}^{\u2032})(0.40) \\\\ 0.12 & =0.06+0.40P(D|{A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 0.40P(D|{A}^{\u2032}) & =0.06 \\\\ P(D|{A}^{\u2032}) & =0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.15.$",
